    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d454">

                <pb facs="0876" n="858" xml:id="pg858"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">501.BC Atomic/6–2746</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by Mr. James E. Doyle, Assistant to the
                        Counselor</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Cohen</hi>), <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Under Secretary of State</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Acheson</hi>)<anchor n="7" xml:id="d454-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-06-27">June 27, 1946</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>I think the most important thing by far is that the current negotiations on the
                    UN Atomic Energy Commission should be held strictly to exploring the nature of
                    the problem of the control of atomic energy. This means that the discussions
                    should be directed to the functions of an international authority on atomic
                    energy. It seems to me unwise even to branch out to the enforcement problem now,
                    and clearly unwise to branch out from atomic energy to the entire field of
                    disarmament.</p>
                <p> SPA’s views on the telegram (through Mr. Blaisdell) are that the USSR has not in
                    fact seized the moral leadership, that inspection is probably not the “basic
                    issue” presently, that the USSR has not grasped the initiative, that the United
                    States may not desire to agree to “unhampered inspection of military
                    establishments and means of production”, and that the Embassy in Moscow may be
                    unable to evaluate more than “the one vital factor” to which it refers.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d454-f1" n="7" type="source" xml:id="d454-f1-content">
                <p>The source text bears the following handwritten addition by Mr. Doyle: “This
                    refers to Moscow’s 2013 of June 27, 1946.”</p>
            </note>
